      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Heavy boots and rolling equipment can cause rapid micro-cracking and deep etching in natural stone, while polished concrete is highly susceptible to staining and becoming abrasively dull if not properly sealed. Stone may also develop dry or uneven patches over time where machinery scours the surface, whereas concrete can sometimes actually become harder with continued foot traffic if the grit bond is robust, though it risks developing epoxied patches that need professional removal. Consequently, both require rigorous cleaning protocols, but stone generally needs more frequent and expensive professional resurfacing to maintain its original aesthetic integrity.",
